.TH LS 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
ls \- list directory contents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ls
.RB [ \-1aClF ]
.RI [ path ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ls
lists what is in each directory named, and names each file named (a file
lists itself). With no
.IR path ,
it lists the current directory. Files named on the command line come
first, then each directory; with more than one
.IR path ,
each directory's list is headed by its name and a colon. Names are listed
in the order the directory gives them (sorted).
.PP
Names starting with a dot are left out unless
.B \-a
is given.
.PP
When standard output is a terminal, names are laid out in columns, filled
down then across, as many as fit the terminal's width; directories show in
bold blue and symbolic links in bold cyan. Into a pipe or a file, names
go one a line, without colors.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be combined
.RB ( \-la )
and may come anywhere among the paths.
.TP
.B \-1
One name a line.
.TP
.B \-a
Include names starting with a dot.
.TP
.B \-C
Columns, even when output is not a terminal (then 80 columns wide).
.TP
.B \-l
Long format: a type letter
.RB ( d
directory,
.B l
symbolic link,
.B c
device,
.B p
pipe,
.B \-
file), the size in bytes, and the name; a link's target follows
.BR \-> .
A link named on the command line is shown as itself, not as what it
points to.
.TP
.B \-F
Append
.B /
to directories and
.B @
to symbolic links.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 2 on a bad option, or if any path could not be read.
.SH NOTES
There are no owners, permissions or times to show, so
.B \-l
gives only the type, the size and the name. A symbolic link to nothing
is listed as the link.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tree (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR ln (1)
